# Stack

## Languages
- Backend:  Rust (stable, pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`) — Cargo workspace
- Frontend: Askama + HTMX, served by the backend. No Node build step.

## Commands

### Build
- Debug:    `cargo build --workspace --all-targets`
- Release:  `cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`

### Test
- All:      `cargo test --workspace`
- Property: `cargo test --workspace -- --include-ignored`

### Type Check / Lint
- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`

### Format
- Apply: `cargo fmt --all`
- Check: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`

## Quality Tools (install if missing)

Each analyzer below MUST conform to the Analyzer Contract: accepts a path,
emits a single JSON document to stdout (`tool`, `metric`, `threshold`,
`violations`, optional `summary`), exits 0 on pass and non-zero on any
violation.

No Rust tool emits the contract natively. Each `Run` is a wrapper under
`scripts/analyzers/` that reshapes the underlying tool's output.

- Coverage:    cargo-llvm-cov
  Install:     `rustup component add llvm-tools-preview && cargo install cargo-llvm-cov`
  Run:         `scripts/analyzers/coverage.sh <path>`
  Threshold:   80  # minimum coverage (%)

- Mutation:    cargo-mutants (runs tests via cargo-nextest)
  Install:     `cargo install cargo-mutants cargo-nextest`
  Run:         `scripts/analyzers/mutation.sh <path>`
  Threshold:   90  # minimum kill rate (%)
  Scope:       scheduler-core and trellis-server only. `crates/acceptance-tests`
               is excluded in `.cargo/mutants.toml`: its mutants are assertion
               helpers and Gherkin step dispatchers, so killing them means
               testing our tests. Gherkin acceptance mutation covers that layer
               first-order. This is half of all mutants and ~90% of the cost.
  Test tool:   nextest, with the `mutants` profile in `.config/nextest.toml`.
               It runs test binaries concurrently instead of one after another
               and stops at the first failure. It also shrinks the baseline
               test time, and cargo-mutants derives its per-mutant timeout from
               that (5x, floored at 20s) -- so a hung mutant is found in ~20s
               rather than ~268s.
  Build cache: `target-mutants/`, set by the analyzer. Never the normal
               `target/`. cargo-mutants tests a patched *copy* of the tree, so
               sharing one cache leaves mutated artifacts behind that later
               `cargo test` runs link against -- a red suite with a clean
               `git status`.
  Timeouts:    not counted as kills. A timeout means the suite did not finish,
               which is a fact about the machine, not the mutant. The analyzer
               reports them separately and fails on them.
  Parallelism: scheduler-core: 8   |   every other crate: 1
               Established, not a default, and do not raise it. `-j > 1` gives
               each mutant its own build directory, so each build is cold:
               cheap for the small pure crate, ruinous for trellis-server's
               axum/sqlx/tokio tree, where it produced timeouts on every
               mutant. Those crates get their speed from the warm shared
               `target-mutants/` cache instead, which requires `-j 1`.

- Complexity:  rust-code-analysis-cli
  Install:     `cargo install rust-code-analysis-cli`
  Run:         `scripts/analyzers/complexity.sh <path>`
  Threshold:   8   # max cyclomatic complexity per function

- CRAP:        custom — composes the coverage and complexity analyzers
  Install:     none
  Run:         `scripts/analyzers/crap.sh <path>`
  Threshold:   30  # exclusive upper bound per function

- DRY:         jscpd
  Install:     `npm i -g jscpd`
  Run:         `scripts/analyzers/dry.sh <path>`
  Threshold:   3   # max % duplicated tokens
  Scope:       product code only -- the acceptance harness is excluded.
               Settled by the owner 2026-08-24. The gate exists to catch
               copy-pasted product logic; 62% of what it was flagging was
               duplication *between* per-screen step modules in
               `crates/acceptance-tests/src/steps/`, which is structural to a
               per-feature harness and not extractable. Measured at the time:
               2.09% product-only against 3.54% including the harness, so the
               threshold keeps biting and did not need moving. Inline
               `#[cfg(test)]` tests stay measured -- they live in product
               files.

- Lint:        clippy
  Install:     `rustup component add clippy`
  Run:         `scripts/analyzers/lint.sh <path>`
  Threshold:   0   # zero warnings, zero errors

## Additional Verification
- Tests:       all pass
- Format:      `cargo fmt --all -- --check` is clean
- Core purity: `cargo tree -p scheduler-core` contains no `tokio` and no `sqlx`
